{"description":"eka-cli vendors the eka-standard declaration (EKA) and the git hook templates into its own tree and embeds them into the binary, but nothing linked those bytes to the standard release they came from. The existing tests assert only internal consistency: the declaration parses, its Version X.Y line is on line 2, and that line equals exchange.SpecificationVersion. A hand-edited copy that keeps a valid Version line satisfies every assertion while its content diverges from the published standard. Nothing refreshed the vendored copy either: scripts/platform-sync.sh prefers a sibling eka-standard checkout and falls back to the vendored bytes, which makes the vendored copy a live fallback path rather than dead weight.","impact":"A silent divergence ships in every generated repository, and the hook templates had no test at all on either side, so a behaviour change in the standard never reached scaffolding repositories. Fix: record the sha256 of each vendored artifact in vendored.yaml together with the eka-standard release it corresponds to, assert the digests in a test that names the release on failure, assert that every embedded artifact is covered by the pin, and provide scripts/pin-vendored so refreshing the copy and re-pinning it is one operation. The pin does not detect a simultaneous edit of file and digest; review covers that, and the pin makes accidental drift loud, which is the failure mode that actually occurs."}